INSTRUCTIONS FOR BUILDING THE TREE16 LIBRARIES

1 - Unpack the compressed tar file.  You've probably done that already
if you're reading this, but if not, you'll need the gnu version of zcat,
or gunzip.  tree15.tgz is a gzip'ed tar file.  If gunzip and tar are in
your path, do:

gunzip -c tree16.tgz | tar xvfo -

This will create a directory ./tree16.<something>/ and a number of subdirectories.

2 - Build the libraries.  You must use the GNU version of make.  I'll
call it gnumake, but it could be called make or Make or
/usr/local/gnu/bin/make or almost anything else...

Change directory to the top of the source tree:

cd tree16.<something>

Next, set the environment variable TREEHOME to the directory you are
now in.  Something like:

setenv TREEHOME `pwd`

will probably work, but some people alias pwd to strange things.  If you're
one of those people, be careful.

Also, add the directory $TREEHOME/bin to your path.  This may not be
necessary, but for some architectures, the calling conventions for the
cross-compilers require extensive environment setting which is handled
by scripts in the bin directory.  If, for example, you see a lot of:
"Paragon:  command not found" error messages coming from 'make', that
means you forgot to set the path appropriately.  You may also need to
correct some of the explicit paths in the scripts (currently: Paragon,
delta, gamma, t3d).

Then build the libraries:

gnumake ARCH=<archname> PAROS=<osname>

Usually, you can leave off the PAROS=<osname>, unless you want to link with
an alternative OS, e.g., sunmos.  You can also leave off ARCH= if there's
an environment variable ARCH that is set to the right thing.  For the
paragon, you would do:

gnumake ARCH=paragon

This will create the directory Objfiles/paragon, and put several .a and
.o files in it.  These are searched when building the application.

Compiler flags in the Makefiles are frequently incomplete or
out-of-date.  If you need to change the compiler flags, look in
Make-common/Make.<archname>.  It should be obvious what to change.

After building the libraries, it will attempt to build the
the "application" code, in the directory relerr/.

You can use any ARCH and/or PAROS that you've built libraries for.  If you
try to build the application before you build the libraries you'll get a slew
of unresolved externals when it tries to link.  Since .o and .a files 
are kept in separate sub-directories, you can freely switch back and
forth between different architectures.  E.g., you can immediately follow
gnumake ARCH=sun4
with 
gnumake ARCH=paragon
without worrying about 'clean'ing any old sun4 object files.

4 - Test it:

Running

relerr.<archname> tst10k_noio.ctl

should run the code with 10k particles, which is small enough to
finish reasonably quickly on a single node (about 50sec on a 486 linux
box).  Do whatever it takes to execute the program on multiple nodes
to test the parallel behavior.

Once the noio version works, try the tst10k.ctl control file, which will
attempt to write out its results.

An assortment of additional control (.ctl) files are present in the
directory.  The obvious parameters to play with are "datafile" (the
file to get input from), errtol (the error tolerance in the force
evaluation functions) and, maybe npart (the number of particles to
generate internally).  One "feature" is that if the datafile starts
with the four letters "test", then it generates the data internally.
Otherwise it reads from the named file.  There should be a more
obvious way to accomplish this, but it's what we've got for now.

Once things are ok for internally generated data, try reading back
one of the data files generated on a previous run.  These should be
independent of the number of processors run on, i.e., you should be
able to read into 32 procs a file that was created on 8.

Debugging:

This may be hopeless, but if you're up for a challenge, you can give
it a try.  There is a built-in mechanism for obtaining diagnostics on
a per-source-file basis.  Just change the line in the .ctl file to,
e.g.:

char Msg_turn_on[]="main_nv.c,cofm_n.c";

and you'll get a printout from every Msgf() call in those two source files.
Beware.  This can be extraordinarily slow (and even fatal) for certain
well-known  parallel OS.  Things are a little better if you restrict messages
to certain nodes, e.g., :
char Msg_turn_on[]="main_nv.c:0,cofm_n.c:0-4";

will only produce messages from main_nv.c on node 0 and cofm_n.c on nodes
0 through 4.

These messages go to files misc.programname/msg.nnn.  If you want them
to go somewhere else, you can give an additional argv on the command line,
or you can set the variable something like:
char msgbase[]="msg_dir/msg";
in the .ctl file.




